{"name":"Crystal Gem","hex":"#79d0a7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crystal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A solid composed of an array of atoms or molecules possessing long-range order and arranged in a pattern which is periodic in three dimensions.","A piece of glimmering, shining mineral resembling ice or glass."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of crystal.","Very clear; coherent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crystal"},{"term":"gem","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-5 language code for Germanic languages."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A precious stone, usually of substantial monetary value or prized for its beauty or shine.","Any precious or highly valued thing or person."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gem"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crystal","blocks":[{"text":"A crystal or crystalline solid is a solid material whose constituents are arranged in a highly ordered microscopic structure, forming a crystal lattice that extends in all directions. In addition, macroscopic single crystals are usually identifiable by their geometrical shape, consisting of flat faces with specific, characteristic orientations. The scientific study of crystals and crystal formation is known as crystallography. The process of crystal formation via mechanisms of crystal growth is called crystallization or solidification."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Quartz_28.jpg/330px-Quartz_28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal","wikidata":"Q43533"},{"title":"Gemstone","blocks":[{"text":"A gemstone is a piece of mineral crystal which, when cut or polished, is used to make jewelry or other adornments. Certain rocks and occasionally organic materials that are not minerals may also be used for jewelry and are therefore often considered to be gemstones as well. Most gemstones are hard, but some softer minerals such as brazilianite may be used in jewelry because of their color, luster, or other physical properties that have aesthetic value. However, generally speaking, soft minerals are not typically used as gemstones by virtue of their brittleness and lack of durability."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Cardinal_gems.png/330px-Cardinal_gems.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemstone","wikidata":"Q83437"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png/500px-Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png/500px-Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Selpologne.jpg/500px-Selpologne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Selpologne.jpg/1280px-Selpologne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Selpologne.jpg/500px-Selpologne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASelpologne.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Ice_crystals.jpg/500px-Ice_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Ice_crystals.jpg/1280px-Ice_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnai